# AI Course Study Material Generator — Multi-Agent RAG Platform

## Project Objective

Build a complete, production-ready AI-powered Course Study Material Generator using a Multi-Agent System, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) validation.

The platform must transform uploaded course documents and syllabus information into structured, accurate, comprehensive study materials through an end-to-end AI workflow.

The system must support multiple AI model providers, automatic model switching when token limits or provider quotas are reached, task recovery, human review, and multiple downloadable study-material formats, including audio.

The platform must function as a reliable AI content-generation system, not merely a chatbot.

---

## 1. Course Details Input

The user must be able to create a course generation project by providing:

- Course Name
- Course Duration
- Course Description
- Syllabus / Course Structure
- High-Level Topics
- Learning Objectives
- Target Audience / Student Level
- Preferred Study Material Language
- Preferred Content Depth
- Negative Prompt / Content Restrictions
- Uploaded Reference Documents

### Supported Reference Documents

- PDF
- DOCX
- PPTX
- TXT
- Markdown
- Scanned documents with OCR support

The negative prompt must be respected throughout the entire generation workflow.

Examples:
- Avoid irrelevant topics.
- Do not include unsupported facts.
- Avoid unnecessary repetition.
- Do not use overly complex explanations.
- Do not generate content outside the syllabus.
- Avoid a specific writing style or content format.

---

## 2. End-to-End AI Workflow

Implement the complete workflow:

**Upload Course Details → Analyze Syllabus → Build Knowledge Base → Generate Study Materials → AI Validation → Human-in-the-Loop Review → Final Output**

Each stage must have a clearly defined state, output, validation status, and recovery mechanism.

### Stage 1 — Course Initialization

Create a course generation project and store all submitted course details.

The system must create a unique project ID and maintain its complete generation history.

### Stage 2 — Document Processing

Process all uploaded documents.

- Extract text and document structure.
- Detect headings, chapters, sections, tables, and page numbers.
- Perform OCR for scanned documents.
- Identify duplicate documents.
- Extract document metadata.
- Store source references.

### Stage 3 — Syllabus Analysis Agent

Analyze the syllabus and course details.

The agent must:

- Understand the complete course structure.
- Identify modules, chapters, and subtopics.
- Extract learning objectives.
- Identify important and prerequisite topics.
- Detect missing syllabus information.
- Create a structured syllabus hierarchy.
- Build a chapter-wise generation plan.

The output must be structured and saved before material generation begins.

### Stage 4 — RAG Knowledge Base

Build a course-specific knowledge base using uploaded reference documents.

Requirements:

- Semantic chunking.
- Embedding generation.
- Vector database storage.
- Metadata-based filtering.
- Hybrid retrieval using vector and keyword search.
- Course, chapter, topic, document, and page-level references.
- Source-grounded retrieval.

The knowledge base must prevent information from unrelated courses from being mixed into the current course.

### Stage 5 — Multi-Agent Study Material Generation

Use a specialized multi-agent workflow.

#### Course Planner Agent
Creates the chapter-wise generation plan and determines the required content.

#### RAG Research Agent
Retrieves relevant source information and provides evidence with document references.

#### Study Material Writer Agent
Generates structured study notes, explanations, definitions, examples, and summaries.

#### Question Generator Agent
Creates MCQs, short-answer questions, long-answer questions, and practice questions.

#### Exam Preparation Agent
Identifies important topics, revision points, and exam-oriented content.

#### Quality Reviewer Agent
Reviews the generated content for accuracy, completeness, relevance, readability, and source grounding.

Agents must have clear responsibilities and structured inputs and outputs.

Avoid unnecessary agent communication. Use controlled workflows and shared state.

---

## 3. AI Validation System

Implement a dedicated AI validation stage before human review.

The validation system must evaluate:

- Factual accuracy
- Source grounding
- Syllabus coverage
- Topic completeness
- Content relevance
- Duplicate content
- Incorrect or unsupported statements
- Question and answer consistency
- MCQ option quality
- Correct answer validation
- Language quality
- Negative prompt compliance
- Content structure and formatting

The validation agent must produce a structured validation report containing:

- Validation status
- Quality score
- Detected issues
- Missing information
- Source verification results
- Recommended corrections

If validation fails, the workflow must route the content back to the appropriate generation agent for correction.

Implement configurable validation thresholds and a maximum retry limit.

If the content still fails validation, mark it as requiring human intervention instead of endlessly regenerating it.

---

## 4. Human-in-the-Loop Review System

Human review is mandatory before final publication.

Implement a complete review interface where the administrator or authorized reviewer can inspect generated study materials.

The reviewer must be able to:

- View generated content chapter by chapter.
- View source references and supporting documents.
- View AI validation reports.
- Edit generated content.
- Approve content.
- Reject content.
- Request regeneration.
- Add review comments.
- Approve individual chapters.
- Approve the complete course.
- Compare original and revised content.

### Review Workflow

Generated Content → AI Validation → Human Review → Approved / Rejected / Revision Required

The workflow must support:

- Pending Review
- Approved
- Rejected
- Revision Required
- Regenerating
- Published

Rejected or revision-requested content must return to the appropriate generation stage with reviewer feedback.

Human approval must be recorded with reviewer identity, timestamp, and revision history.

The system must never publish unapproved content when human approval is required.

---

## 5. Multi-Provider AI Model Management

Implement a centralized AI Model Provider Management system.

The platform must support multiple AI providers and model APIs, including:

- Google Gemini
- OpenAI
- OpenAI-compatible API providers
- OpenCode-compatible model integrations where supported
- OpenRouter or other configurable model aggregators
- Additional providers through extensible adapters

Do not hardcode a single AI provider.

### Provider Configuration

Allow administrators to configure:

- Provider name
- API endpoint
- API key
- Available models
- Model capabilities
- Context window
- Maximum output tokens
- Token or usage limits
- Provider priority
- Cost configuration
- Rate limits
- Enabled / Disabled status

API credentials must be encrypted and securely managed.

### Intelligent Model Routing

Implement a Model Router that selects the best available model based on:

- Task type
- Model capability
- Context length
- Token availability
- Provider availability
- Cost
- Quality requirements
- Configured priority

Use different models for different tasks when appropriate.

For example:

- Syllabus analysis → reasoning-capable model
- Study notes generation → high-quality text model
- MCQ generation → structured-output-capable model
- Content validation → independent review model
- Translation → multilingual model

### Automatic Token / Quota Fallback

If the current model reaches its token limit, quota limit, rate limit, or provider usage restriction:

1. Detect the provider error.
2. Determine whether the failure is temporary or quota-related.
3. Select the next eligible configured provider or model.
4. Preserve the current task state.
5. Resume the failed task using the existing inputs and outputs.
6. Continue generation without restarting the entire course.
7. Record the provider switch in the execution history.

The system must support automatic provider switching for the same task.

Do not lose previously generated chapters or completed stages.

Implement retry policies, exponential backoff, provider health checks, circuit breakers, and task checkpoints.

Do not retry indefinitely for invalid API keys, unsupported models, or permanent configuration errors.

---

## 6. Task Persistence and Recovery

Use persistent workflow state.

Every generation task must maintain:

- Project ID
- Course ID
- Chapter ID
- Current workflow stage
- Current agent
- Selected model and provider
- Task status
- Input references
- Generated output
- Validation results
- Retry count
- Provider fallback history
- Human review status

If the application restarts, a provider fails, or generation is interrupted, the system must resume from the last successful checkpoint.

The system must support:

- Pause generation
- Resume generation
- Cancel generation
- Retry failed tasks
- Regenerate individual chapters
- Regenerate selected sections
- Continue with another model provider

Use background task execution for long-running generation jobs.

---

## 7. Study Material Output

Generate structured study materials based on the course syllabus.

The output must include:

### Course-Level Materials
- Course overview
- Complete syllabus
- Learning objectives
- Course summary
- Study roadmap

### Chapter-Level Materials
- Chapter introduction
- Detailed study notes
- Topic-wise explanations
- Important definitions
- Examples
- Key concepts
- Summary
- Revision notes
- Important questions

### Examination Materials
- MCQs
- MCQ answers with explanations
- Short-answer questions
- Long-answer questions
- Practice tests
- Important topics
- Revision checklist

All content must maintain consistent formatting and chapter structure.

---

## 8. Multiple Download Formats

Provide downloadable study materials in multiple formats.

Required formats:

- PDF
- DOCX
- Markdown
- HTML
- TXT
- JSON

Allow users to download:

- Complete course materials
- Individual chapters
- Selected sections
- Question banks
- MCQ sets
- Revision notes
- Approved final content

Generated files must preserve headings, tables, lists, question numbering, and source references wherever applicable.

Only approved content should be included in final published downloads.

---

## 9. Audio Study Materials

Generate audio versions of approved study materials.

The system must support:

- Chapter-wise audio
- Topic-wise audio
- Full-course audio
- Audio summaries
- Revision audio
- Multiple language support
- Configurable voice and speaking speed

Use a suitable Text-to-Speech provider to generate audio.

Required audio output formats:

- MP3
- WAV

Store audio files and associate them with the relevant course, chapter, and content revision.

Support audio generation as a separate background task so that audio failures do not interrupt text generation.

Optionally support Whisper-based speech-to-text for voice input, transcription, or spoken student feedback.

---

## 10. Technology Architecture

Use the following technology stack:

### Backend
- Python
- Django
- Django REST Framework

### AI Agent System
- LangGraph for multi-agent orchestration
- LangChain for LLM integrations and tools
- Structured outputs and typed state management

### RAG
- Qdrant vector database
- PostgreSQL full-text search
- Embedding models
- Hybrid retrieval
- Document metadata and source tracking

### Background Processing
- Celery
- Redis

### File Processing
- PyMuPDF
- python-docx
- python-pptx
- OCR integration

### Frontend
- Modern responsive web application
- Course management dashboard
- Generation progress dashboard
- AI validation report interface
- Human review interface
- Download and export interface

### Storage
- PostgreSQL for application data
- Object storage or filesystem abstraction for documents, exports, and audio

---

## 11. Dashboard and Monitoring

Create a dashboard showing:

- Total courses
- Active generation projects
- Completed projects
- Failed tasks
- Pending human reviews
- Approved materials
- Provider usage
- Token consumption
- Model switching history
- Generation progress
- Validation scores

Provide detailed logs for every agent execution and provider request.

Logs must be useful for debugging without exposing API keys or sensitive document content unnecessarily.

---

## 12. Important System Requirements

- Modular and maintainable architecture.
- Proper database relationships.
- Secure API key management.
- Course-level data isolation.
- Reliable background processing.
- Persistent workflow state.
- Automatic provider fallback.
- Human approval before publication.
- Source-grounded RAG generation.
- Structured agent outputs.
- Configurable retry and validation policies.
- No unnecessary agent loops.
- No loss of completed generation work.
- Responsive and professional UI.
- Clear error handling and execution logs.

## Final Development Goal

Build a complete, functional, end-to-end AI Course Study Material Generator.

The platform must allow a user to submit course details and reference documents, automatically analyze the syllabus, generate comprehensive study materials using multiple specialized AI agents and RAG, validate the content, send it through a human review workflow, and produce approved downloadable documents and audio materials.

The system must support multiple AI model providers and automatically switch to another available model when token limits, quotas, or provider restrictions prevent the current model from continuing.

Prioritize reliability, content accuracy, workflow persistence, source verification, and human-controlled publication.

Do not build only a demo or a basic chatbot. Implement the complete system with a production-oriented architecture, working backend workflows, database persistence, provider management, review controls, and a usable web interface.